We investigate Turing instability in a reaction-diffusion system with a nonlocal, non-conservative diffusion mechanism. The system's dynamics are described by the equations:

\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
\partial_t u = \partial_x^2 u + u - uv + \int_{\mathbb{R}} u(y,t) dy - u, \\
\partial_t v = \partial_x^2 v - uv + \int_{\mathbb{R}} v(y,t) dy - v,
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}

where $u$ and $v$ are non-negative functions defined on $\mathbb{R} \times [0, T)$ for some $T > 0$. The system's behavior is analyzed in the Fourier domain, revealing that Turing instability occurs when the system's eigenvalues have positive real parts. The analysis shows that the system's behavior is influenced by the diffusion coefficients and the system's parameters.

This research focuses on the existence of non-trivial solutions for the double phase problem 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
-\operatorname{div}\left(|\nabla u|^{p-2}\nabla u+\left|\nabla u\right|^{q-2}\nabla u\right)=\left|u\right|^{\alpha}_{+}u+\left|u\right|^{q-2}u\quad\mbox{in }\mathbb{R}^{N},\\
u\in W^{1,p}\left(\mathbb{R}^{N}\right)\cap L^{q}\left(\mathbb{R}^{N}\right),
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
where $1<p<q$, $\alpha>-1$, and the positive part of $u$ is denoted by $u_{+}$. The study leverages the concentration-compactness principle and the mountain pass theorem to establish the existence of a non-trivial solution.
